    <h2>1. The NDS Fastener Withdrawal Formula</h2>
    <p>
      Per the <em>National Design Specification (NDS) for Wood Construction</em> Section 12.2, the reference withdrawal design value (W) for a lag screw inserted into the side grain of seasoned wood is calculated using the formula:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-600 p-4 font-mono text-sm text-blue-900 my-4">
      W = 1,800 x (G)^(3/2) x D^(3/4) lbs per inch of thread penetration
    </div>
    <p>
      Where:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>G:</strong> Specific gravity of the framing lumber species (oven-dry basis).</li>
      <li><strong>D:</strong> Unthreaded nominal shank diameter of the lag screw (inches).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Common Framing Lumber Species &amp; 5/16" Lag Capacity Table</h2>
    <p>
      The load a lag screw can hold depends directly on the wood species used in your garage ceiling framing:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Wood Species Group</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Specific Gravity (G)</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Allowable Withdrawal (5/16" Lag / Inch)</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Allowable Capacity (2" Thread Embedment)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Southern Yellow Pine (SYP)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">0.55</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">292 lbs / inch</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">584 lbs per screw</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
