          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>3. The Dangerous Myth: Roof Truss Bottom Chords are NOT Attic Floors</h2>
    <p>
      Many homeowners assume that because their garage roof has pre-fabricated 2x4 wooden trusses, they can hang unlimited weight from the ceiling. This is an extremely dangerous structural assumption:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tension Only Design:</strong> Light wood roof trusses (ANSI/TPI 1) are engineered primarily to carry roof live loads (snow, wind) through the diagonal webbing. The horizontal bottom chord exists mainly in <em>tension</em> to keep the exterior walls from spreading apart.</li>
      <li><strong>Dead Load Allowance:</strong> Standard residential garage trusses are engineered for a bottom-chord dead load of only <strong>5 to 10 pounds per square foot (PSF)</strong>—just enough to support 1/2-inch gypsum ceiling drywall, light fixtures, and garage door tracks.</li>
      <li><strong>Span Spreading Rule:</strong> A 4x8 overhead storage rack loaded to its 600 lb maximum generates a localized load of 18.75 PSF! If you anchor all four ceiling brackets along a single truss, you create a severe point-load that can cause the truss bottom chord to deflect, crack interior drywall, or pop truss gusset plates.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. How to Correctly Anchor Ceiling Racks Across Multiple Trusses</h2>
    <p>
      To prevent localized truss overloading, always follow these three structural guidelines:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Mount Perpendicular:</strong> Orient the long 8-foot dimension of the rack perpendicular to the direction of the roof trusses. For 24-inch on-center truss spacing, an 8-foot rack spans across <strong>5 separate trusses</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Distribute Fasteners:</strong> Install brackets so each individual ceiling truss carries no more than 100 to 125 lbs of suspended load.</li>
      <li><strong>Add Spanner 2x4 Backers if Parallel:</strong> If you must mount the rack parallel to trusses, install 2x6 wood spanner blocks screwed flat across three adjacent trusses in the attic, and drive your rack lag screws directly into the spanners.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

