    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Why Modern Structural Wood Screws Outperform Traditional Hex Lag Bolts"
    note="Traditional 5/16-inch hex lag bolts have been used for decades, but they carry a high failure rate during DIY installation: overtightening with an impact socket often strips out the soft wood threads, or under-drilling snaps the hex head clean off. In modern structural engineering, we almost exclusively specify specialized structural timber screws (such as GRK RSS, Simpson Strong-Tie SDWS, or FastenMaster TimberLOK). These fasteners are manufactured from heat-treated, hardened carbon steel. They feature a patented knurled 'CEKOR' shank, a self-tapping Type 17 cutting tip that virtually eliminates wood splitting, and a built-in washer head with deep star-drive (Torx) recesses. A 5/16-inch structural timber screw delivers equal or higher withdrawal holding power than a traditional lag, can be driven directly without a separate washer, and never snaps in dry lumber."
  />

